  <paragraph>Now we’ll factor the greatest common factor from a trinomial. We start by finding the GCF of all three terms.</paragraph>
  <example number="7.8">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Factor: $$4y^{2} + 24y + 28$$ .</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <paragraph>We start by finding the GCF of all three terms.</paragraph>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell>Find the GCF of $$4y^{2}$$ , $$24y$$ and 28.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Rewrite each term as a product using the GCF.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Factor the GCF.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Check by mulitplying.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>$$4\left( y^{2} + 6y + 7 \right)$$</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>$$4 \cdot y^{2} + 4 \cdot 6y + 4 \cdot 7$$</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>$$4y^{2} + 24y + 28✓$$</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
